  <section id="how-to-read">
    <h2>How to read this page</h2>
    <p>
      This is a short, unsigned profile of Samkough. Unlike most of the other
      articles on this site, it is about him alone, and it adds details I
      haven’t seen elsewhere. It is also someone else’s published work, so I
      have <strong>not</strong> translated it in full. This page summarizes it
      in my own words and translates a few short passages. Please have a Turkish
      reader check the translations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mark>Highlighted text marked “Review”</mark> is something I’m unsure of
      or that disagrees with another source on this site. The article gives no
      references at all, so <strong>every detail below is unsourced</strong>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="summary">
    <h2>Summary</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Birth and family.</strong> Born in 1900 in the village of
        Mansure (Mansura) in the Golan, which the article notes is “still under
        Israeli occupation”. His grandfather was exiled from the Caucasus in
        1864, first to the Balkans and then to Syria, and was a village headman
        (<span lang="tr">«muhtar»</span>). His father, Eyüp Semguğ, fought with
        Ottoman forces defending Libya (Tripolitania) against the Italian
        attack in 1911.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Education.</strong> Primary school in his village and in
        Quneitra, in Arabic. Secondary school in Damascus and Beirut, in
        Turkish. He learned French during two years in Beirut. He then went to
        France and studied at the “L’École Nationale de Grignon” and the
        “L’École des Sciences Politiques”, earning two diplomas, in general
        agriculture and in international politics.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Émigré politics.</strong> From France he kept in touch with
        Caucasian émigré organizations and publications in Paris, Berlin, Prague
        and Warsaw, and helped link them with Caucasian émigrés in the Arab
        countries and Turkey. He held posts in the “Caucasus Mountaineers’
        People’s Party” (the article gives the Russian name,
        <span lang="ru">Народная партия горцев Кавказа</span>, in Latin
        letters) and kept this up after returning to Syria.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public office.</strong> He held some administrative posts and
        was mayor of Quneitra. No dates are given.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The school.</strong> He arranged the opening of a middle-school
        level Circassian school in Quneitra, where girls and boys studied
        together. He planned to develop it into an agricultural vocational
        school, and worked for branches in Reyhaniye (near Antakya) and in the
        Quneitra villages of Hışniye and Adnaniye.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The 1937 company.</strong> To draw Syria’s Circassians away from
        French colonial influence and from civil-service and military careers,
        toward farming and trade, he had a company founded with local capital,
        transliterated as <span lang="tr">«Şirketiccevlan-etticariyye»</span>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The newspaper.</strong> He helped publish the newspaper
        <em>Marg</em> in Quneitra, in Circassian, French, Arabic and Turkish
        (1928–31), and wrote articles and poems for it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Character.</strong> Spoke Adyghe, French, Arabic and Turkish
        very well. In his spare time he played the harmonica and sang Circassian
        laments. He is described as very modest, as someone who listened to
        everyone’s troubles and looked for remedies, and as having made a
        significant contribution to the social, political and cultural
        development of Syria’s Circassians. He also helped calm disputes and
        clashes between Circassians and Druze.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Death.</strong> 1952.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
