  <section id="review">
    <h2>Things to review</h2>

    <h3>Conflicts with other sources on this site</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>life dates.</strong> This page gives 1900 and 1952.
          The Mirzoev articles and the dictionary entry give 1900–1953, and
          the <a href="/people/amin-samkough/biography/">biography page</a>
          and the handwritten note on his portrait give 1903–1952. This page
          mixes the two sets.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>birthplace.</strong> This page says the village of
          Mansure in the Golan. The dictionary entry says Quneitra, and Mirzoev
          says only “in Syria”. Both may be right, if the village is in the
          Quneitra district. I haven’t verified the village.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>where he studied agriculture and politics.</strong>
          Here it is two schools in France, Grignon for agriculture and
          the École des Sciences Politiques for politics. Mirzoev says he
          qualified as an agronomist in Beirut and then studied political
          economy at the Sorbonne. The 2022 Jineps translation says
          “agricultural engineering” in Beirut. These can’t all be right as
          written, though he may have studied in both places.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the 1937 organization.</strong> Mirzoev says that in
          1937 he and his associates formed “Jolan”, an organization to promote
          the return of Circassians to the Caucasus, and that a request to the
          Soviet embassy in Paris was refused. This page says a trading company
          was formed in 1937 with local capital to move Circassians toward
          farming and trade. The names match (Golan/Jolan), but the purposes
          are different. They may be one body described two ways, or two
          separate ones.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the newspaper’s dates and languages.</strong> This
          page says 1928–31, in four languages. Mirzoev gives 7 October 1927 to
          1932, also in four languages. Issue no. 71 on the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/marj-newspaper/">Marj page</a> is
          dated 14 August 1931, which is hard to fit with a run that ends in
          1931 unless it was published often, and its front page is in Ottoman
          Turkish with an Arabic box. The article also names no editor, whereas
          Mirzoev names Hagur Tarik Mumtaz.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>his books.</strong> This page puts “Circassians in
          Their Wars against the Tsars” in Homs in 1948. Mirzoev says it
          was published in Amman in 1984, and that the Introduction was
          published in Damascus in 1984. Here the
          Introduction is “after his death, in the 1980s”, with no place. The
          1948 date may be a typo for 1984, but it is stated as written.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the school.</strong> Here it is a middle-school-level
          school with girls and boys together, with planned branches in
          Reyhaniye, Hışniye and Adnaniye. None of this appears in Mirzoev,
          whose version has the school opening in 1933. The article gives no
          year for it, and no mention of the teachers or the anthem.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Details that appear nowhere else on this site</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: his grandfather’s exile route (Balkans, then Syria), the
          grandfather’s role as village headman, and his father’s service in
          Libya in 1911. None is sourced.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: the claim that he mediated between Circassians and Druze.
          It is a single sentence with no date, place or event.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: the personal details (harmonica, laments, modesty). They read
          like praise, not like documented fact.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>spelling.</strong> The headline says “Semsuğ”, but the
          text calls his father “Semguğ”. One is likely a typing slip. The first
          is the odd one out, since “Semguğ” matches the Russian «Самгуг».
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Where it agrees with other sources</h3>
    <p>
      The Mountaineers’ People’s Party role matches the
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/north-caucasians-in-emigration/">2020 dictionary entry</a>,
      which also lists him as a board member. This page is more specific, with
      the cities he kept contact with, and it fits the
      <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/jineps-middle-east-in-the-kdhp-years/">KDHP article</a>
      that mentions Prague and Warsaw. It is still unsourced, and it may be the
      origin of the other accounts, not a confirmation of them.
    </p>
  </section>
